Charge density or charge per unit length of long wire

According to Gauss theorem $\begin{array}{l}
\oint \vec{E} \cdot d \vec{S}=\frac{q}{\varepsilon_{0}}\\
\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow E \oint d S=\frac{q}{\varepsilon_{0}} \text{ or } E \times2 \pi r l=\frac{q}{\varepsilon_{0}}\\
\Rightarrow E=\frac{q}{2 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r l}=\frac{q / l}{2 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r}\\
=\frac{\lambda \times 2}{2 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r \times 2}=\frac{\lambda \times 2}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} r}\\
=9 \times10^{9} \times \frac{1}{3} \times2 \times \frac{1}{18 \times 10^{-2}}\\
=0.33 \times10^{11} \mathrm{NC}^{-1}
\end{aligned} 
\end{array}$

Maximum electric field, $E_{\operatorname{mix}}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{Q_{\max }}{R^{2}}\begin{array}{l}
3 \times 10^{6}=\frac{9 \times 10^{9} \times Q_{\max }}{2.5 \times 2.5} \\
\text{ or } \quad Q_{\max }=\frac{3 \times 10^{6} \times 2.5 \times 2.5}{9 \times 10^{9}} \\
=2 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{C}
\end{array}$
